Motivation {#motivation}
==========

A *non-zero indicator* (NZI) is a counter-like object that supports
`arrive`, `depart`, and `query` operations, where `query` returns only
whether the counter is zero or non-zero. Many concurrent-runtime
lifetime predicates reduce to NZIs:

  * "Are there any outstanding references to this object?"
  * "Are there any readers in this critical section?"
  * "Is any thread currently using this hazard pointer?"
  * "Are there any tasks still in flight in this work-stealing scheduler?"
  * "Are there any in-flight callbacks before I disconnect this slot?"

The naive implementation — a single shared `atomic<int>` counter — does
not scale: every `arrive` and `depart` contends on a single cache line,
and the read-mostly workloads typical of NZI clients (many concurrent
arrives and departs, occasional zero-queries) suffer write-write
contention proportional to the level of concurrency.

Several existing C++ standard library facilities encode an
indicator-style lifetime predicate over a counter that does not scale:

  * `std::shared_ptr` strong-count is a single `atomic<long>` and is a
    well-known scalability bottleneck under high contention.
  * `std::shared_mutex` reader-acquisition centralizes on a small set
    of state words and exhibits writer-bias issues that compound the
    contention.
  * Proposed `<rcu>` quiescent-state tracking and `<hazptr>` hazard
    enumeration both reduce to "is anyone here?" queries that
    `atomic<int>` answers with poor scaling characteristics.
  * `std::latch` encodes the same arrive/wait pattern but is one-shot:
    it cannot re-arm. Many lifetime-predicate use cases need a
    re-armable latch.
  * The `operation_state` child-completion predicate in [[P2300]]
    "Senders and Receivers" is naturally a non-zero indicator over an
    asymmetric (potentially many-spawn, single-await) workload.

[[ELLM07]] introduced *Scalable Non-Zero Indicators* (SNZIs), which
spread arrive/depart traffic across a tree of per-thread leaf counters
and propagate state changes upward only on transitions that affect the
root indicator. The result is amortized O(1) cost per operation under
contention, with the indicator-state observable through a single
load-acquire on a designated indicator bit.

This paper proposes a single SNZI primitive that can serve as the
substrate for the use cases above, with two API tiers that match how
the primitive is actually used:

  1. A low-level `snzi_indicator<Root>` parameterized on
     caller-supplied storage, allowing the indicator bit to co-locate
     with application data (e.g. packed into the strong-count word of
     a `shared_ptr` control block, or into the state word of a
     `shared_mutex`).
  2. A convenience type `snzi` that owns its own root storage and
     commits to an ABI-stable layout. Drop-in for cases where the
     indicator does not need to co-locate with application state.


Use cases {#usecases}
=========

These are the canonical use cases driving the design. Each is naturally
expressed as an NZI; today each is implemented with mechanisms (raw
`atomic<int>`, ad-hoc tree counters, per-thread state) that are
either non-scalable or duplicative.

<table>
<tr><th>Use case</th><th>Tier</th><th>Why</th></tr>

<tr><td>**Reference counting** (intrusive_ptr / `shared_ptr` strong)</td>
<td>Low-level</td>
<td>Indicator co-located with the strong/weak count word for atomicity
of combined updates.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**Reader/writer locks**</td><td>Low-level</td>
<td>Reader presence packed alongside writer state for combined CAS.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**RCU quiescence** (proposed `<rcu>`)</td><td>Low-level</td>
<td>Indicator co-located with epoch / generation word.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**Hazard-pointer enumeration** (proposed `<hazptr>`)</td><td>Low-level</td>
<td>Indicator per hazard slot, co-located with slot state.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**Work-stealing termination detection**</td><td>Convenience</td>
<td>Standalone counter; Cilk/TBB-style spawn/sync. Bulk arrive(n) for
spawn-many.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**P2300 `operation_state` child-completion**</td><td>Convenience</td>
<td>Standalone; naturally fits the `bulk` algorithm's fan-out pattern
([[P2300]]).</td></tr>

<tr><td>**Async I/O drain** (executor / `io_context` shutdown)</td><td>Convenience</td>
<td>Standalone counter of in-flight operations; `wait_until_zero` for
drain.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**`stop_source` / `jthread` cleanup**</td><td>Convenience</td>
<td>Drain in-flight callbacks before destruction.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**Re-armable latches**</td><td>Convenience</td>
<td>`std::latch` cannot re-arm; SNZI's 0→nonzero→0 cycle naturally supports it.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**Connection / handle pool drain**</td><td>Convenience</td>
<td>Standalone "any handles in use?" predicate.</td></tr>

<tr><td>**Telemetry "any in-flight RPCs?" gauges**</td><td>Convenience</td>
<td>Where only zero-vs-nonzero matters; full counter is wasted state.</td></tr>
</table>


Algorithm summary {#algo}
=================

The algorithm is the binary-tree variant from [[ELLM07]]. We provide
the summary needed to follow the API and design discussion; rigorous
correctness arguments are deferred to the cited paper.

A balanced binary tree of counters, leaves at index ⌈N/2⌉..N-1 of an
array, root at index 0. Each thread hashes to a leaf and arrives /
departs there. State changes propagate upward on transitions that
change a node's "is non-zero?" status:

  * **Leaf arrive**: atomic CAS to increment leaf counter. If the
    leaf was zero, the leaf must announce itself non-zero to its
    parent (recursive arrive on the parent), then publish a counter
    value of 1 on the leaf. The intermediate "announced but not yet
    published" state is encoded as a special value `½`.
  * **Leaf depart**: atomic CAS to decrement. If the leaf transitions
    from 1 to 0, recursively depart the parent.
  * **Root arrive/depart**: similar, but the root's transitions
    additionally drive the application-visible indicator bit and
    issue `notify_all` on the indicator-storage word.

A version stamp stored in each tree node guards against an A-B-A
hazard between concurrent arrive (raising the leaf) and depart
(lowering the leaf back to zero).

Amortized cost is O(1) per arrive/depart in the absence of root-
transition contention, with worst-case O(log N) propagation when the
indicator transitions.


Proposed API {#api}
============

Two tiers {#tiers}
---------

```cpp
namespace std {
    // Low-level — caller supplies the Root.
    template<class Root, std::size_t TreeSize = /*default*/>
    class snzi_indicator;

    // Convenience — owns its own root storage.
    class snzi;
}
```

`snzi_indicator<Root, TreeSize>` is the algorithmic primitive, fully
generic over storage. The caller (e.g. a shared_ptr control-block
implementation) supplies a `Root` type that exposes the indicator bit
and a few atomic operations on a word of the caller's choosing. The
indicator bit is thus *co-located* with whatever application state
already lives in that word — no extra storage, no extra cache line.

`snzi` is one specific instantiation of `snzi_indicator` over a
library-supplied default Root, with the storage word owned internally.
The convenience type commits to a specific ABI-stable layout (see
[[#abi]]) and is the right choice when the indicator does not need to
co-locate with application state.


The Root concept {#root}
----------------

A type `Root` models the SNZI Root concept iff it provides:

```cpp
struct Root {
    using version_type = /* unsigned integer, ≥ 16 bits */;

    // Unconditionally publish the indicator value, bumping the version.
    void snzi_write(bool indicator) noexcept;

    // Load the indicator and a version stamp atomically (the LL of LL/SC).
    std::pair<bool, version_type> snzi_load_linked() const noexcept;

    // Conditional store: if version still matches, publish indicator
    // and bump version. Returns true iff store occurred.
    bool snzi_store_conditional(bool indicator,
                                version_type expected) noexcept;

    // Wait/notify hooks (required iff the SNZI client uses wait_*).
    void snzi_notify_indicator() noexcept;
    void snzi_wait_indicator(bool target) const noexcept;
};
```

`snzi_load_linked` returns a `std::pair` rather than a custom struct
specifically to avoid imposing member-name constraints. Call sites use
structured bindings:

```cpp
auto [indicator, version] = root.snzi_load_linked();
```

### Semantic obligations ### {#root-semantics}

  1. `snzi_write(b)` and `snzi_store_conditional(b, _)` must publish
     `b` such that a subsequent `snzi_load_linked()` from a thread
     synchronizing-with the write observes `b`.
  2. The version stamp must change between any two writes. *In
     particular a write of the same indicator value must still bump
     the version.* This is what defends the depart-root LL/SC against
     A-B-A.
  3. `snzi_notify_indicator` must be ordered-after the write that
     transitions the indicator. Implementations may coalesce
     notifications across writes that don't change the indicator
     value, but must not skip a notification on a true transition.
  4. `snzi_wait_indicator(t)` returns when the indicator is observed
     equal to `t`. Spurious wakeups are permitted (per `atomic_wait`
     general semantics); the SNZI implementation re-checks on return.


### Default Root ### {#root-default}

The standard library supplies a default Root that owns its storage
word and lays out the indicator and version bits at parameterizable
positions. A typical instantiation reserves:

  * 1 bit for the indicator
  * 16 bits for the version (free-running mod 2^16)
  * up to 47 bits unallocated (available for application-side packing
    in the low-level tier; unused in the convenience tier)

This default supports both the "I want to use SNZI standalone"
scenario and the "I want to pack the indicator into my existing word"
scenario, with parameterization via template arguments rather than
runtime configuration (so layout choices are fully resolved at
compile time and the bit-manipulation code optimizes well).


Convenience snzi {#snzi}
----------------

```cpp
class snzi {
public:
    snzi() noexcept;
    snzi(const snzi&) = delete;
    snzi& operator=(const snzi&) = delete;
    snzi(snzi&&) = delete;
    snzi& operator=(snzi&&) = delete;
    ~snzi();

    void arrive() noexcept;
    void arrive(std::size_t n) noexcept;

    void depart() noexcept;
    void depart(std::size_t n) noexcept;

    [[nodiscard]] bool query() const noexcept;

    void wait_until_zero() const noexcept;
    void wait_until_nonzero() const noexcept;
};
```

The type is non-copyable and non-movable: active per-leaf state has no
coherent move semantics, and concurrent users would observe undefined
behavior across a move.


Bulk arrive and depart {#bulk}
======================

`arrive(n)` raises the indicator's logical count by `n`. `depart(n)`
lowers it by `n`. Both compose with their unary forms:

```cpp
s.arrive(5);   // equivalent to five s.arrive() calls, but cheaper
s.depart(5);   // equivalent to five s.depart() calls, but cheaper
```

Algorithmic cost: `arrive(n)` and `depart(n)` are no more expensive
than their unary variants. The leaf CAS adds (or subtracts) `n`
instead of 1, and the parent-propagation traffic is unchanged: a
leaf transitioning 0 → n still announces exactly once to its
parent, just as 0 → 1 does. Bulk subsumes n unary calls, never
worse, sometimes strictly better.

The use cases are:

  * **`bulk` algorithm fan-out** in [[P2300]]. A `bulk(n, f)`
    invocation on an `operation_state` predicate spawns n logical
    children; the predicate's arrive should be `arrive(n)`, not n
    separate `arrive()` calls, both for performance and because the
    spawn is logically one event.
  * **Cilk-style spawn-N-children** in work-stealing schedulers.
  * **Batched executor `post_n`**.
  * **Reference counting `copy_n`** when copying a sequence of
    references.

Issue: the bulk-on-leaf-in-½-state case has a subtle correctness
argument (multiple concurrent bulk arrivers race to commit a non-1
value). The freestanding implementation handles this correctly via
the standard helping-and-undo pattern from [[ELLM07]], but the
formal argument deserves a careful write-up here for a future
revision.


Wait and notify {#waitnotify}
===============

The convenience type and the low-level type both expose
`wait_until_zero()` and `wait_until_nonzero()`. These delegate to the
Root's `snzi_wait_indicator` and `snzi_notify_indicator` operations.

Notification is fired exclusively at root transitions (0 → non-zero
or non-zero → 0). Interior tree-node CAS operations and version-bit
updates do not notify. This matters for cost: arrive/depart traffic
through the leaves is bounded only by application concurrency, while
indicator transitions are bounded by the rate at which the underlying
predicate actually flips — typically much rarer (e.g. a `shared_ptr`
strong count flips to zero only on final release).

The natural implementation is built on `atomic_ref::wait` /
`notify_all` (C++20). A predicate-style wait, as proposed in
[[P2643]] "Improving C++ Concurrency Features," yields a much cleaner
implementation:

```cpp
void wait_until_zero() const noexcept {
    storage.wait_predicate([](auto v) {
        return (v & indicator_mask) == 0;
    });
}
```

Without P2643, the same operation is correct but pays a cost in
spurious wakeups: every depart bumps the version stamp, which would
otherwise wake naive `atomic_wait(prev_value)` callers. The SNZI
implementation re-checks on return and re-issues the wait if the
target state is not yet reached.

Recommendation: target P2643 when available, but ensure the SNZI
implementation degrades gracefully on the C++20 baseline so SNZI
ships independent of P2643's standardization timeline.


Memory ordering {#order}
===============

Memory ordering choices are summarized below. Each is the minimum
sufficient for the algorithm; we expect this section to attract
reviewer scrutiny and have prepared explicit defenses for each
choice in the implementation.

<table>
<tr><th>Operation</th><th>Order</th><th>Justification</th></tr>

<tr><td>Leaf load (arrive/depart inner loop)</td><td>relaxed</td>
<td>The CAS provides the synchronization; loading the value to compute
the next CAS does not need ordering.</td></tr>

<tr><td>Leaf CAS, no propagation</td><td>release on success, relaxed
on failure</td><td>Pure counter motion; no inter-thread visibility
requirement until a propagation event.</td></tr>

<tr><td>Leaf CAS that propagates to parent (½ → 1, or 1 → 0)</td>
<td>release on success, relaxed on failure</td><td>The publication
of the leaf's new state must happen-before the parent's
observation.</td></tr>

<tr><td>Root indicator publication
(`snzi_write` / `snzi_store_conditional` success)</td>
<td>release</td><td>Pairs with `query` and `wait_*`
acquires.</td></tr>

<tr><td>`snzi_notify_indicator`</td><td>after release</td>
<td>Sequenced after the publication.</td></tr>

<tr><td>`query`</td><td>acquire</td>
<td>Must observe the publication's release.</td></tr>

<tr><td>`wait_*` recheck</td><td>acquire</td>
<td>Same as `query`.</td></tr>
</table>


ABI considerations {#abi}
==================

Every layout decision in the convenience `snzi` type becomes
ABI-permanent at first ship. Tabulated:

<table>
<tr><th>Decision</th><th>Default (proposed)</th><th>Rationale</th></tr>

<tr><td>Tree size</td><td>32 nodes (16 leaves)</td>
<td>Initial benchmarks (see [[#bench]]) plateau at 16 leaves on a
16-thread machine. Larger-machine data may justify revisiting before
lockdown.</td></tr>

<tr><td>Cacheline padding</td><td>`alignas(64)`</td>
<td>Matches x86-64 / ARMv8 cache lines. Apple Silicon and recent
Intel have effective 128-byte lines. The choice is conservative
(works everywhere) but suboptimal on those targets.</td></tr>

<tr><td>Root word size</td><td>`uint64_t`</td>
<td>Wide enough for indicator + version + caller's data. Lock-free on
all targets that support `atomic<uint64_t>`.</td></tr>

<tr><td>Root word bit layout</td><td>indicator bit 63; version bits
15..0 (16-bit); bits 62..16 owned by application or unused</td>
<td>16-bit version supports realistic write rates without aliasing.
Three reserved bits at 62..60 hold space for future extensions
(lock-bit, drain-bit, weighted-counter mode).</td></tr>

<tr><td>Tree node alignment</td><td>`alignas(64)`</td>
<td>Same as cacheline.</td></tr>

<tr><td>Default leaf-selector</td><td>`hash(this_thread::get_id()) %
leaf_count`</td><td>Hosted-only by default; pluggable for
freestanding (see [[#open]]).</td></tr>

<tr><td>Movability/copyability</td><td>non-movable, non-copyable</td>
<td>Active per-leaf state has no coherent move semantics; concurrent
users would observe UB.</td></tr>
</table>

The low-level `snzi_indicator<Root, TreeSize>` tier exposes layout
choices via the type system: different `TreeSize` values produce
different types, so users who need different choices simply
instantiate their own. There is no ABI clash between e.g.
`snzi_indicator<R, 16>` and `snzi_indicator<R, 32>`.

Three reserved bits in the convenience type's root word (bits 62..60)
provide an extension surface without ABI break. Once any of these is
allocated in a future revision, that allocation becomes ABI-permanent
for the default Root; custom Roots are unaffected.

If the convenience type ever requires an ABI-incompatible change
(e.g. raising the default tree size from 32 to 64 because larger-
machine data justifies it), the libstdc++ inline-namespace versioning
machinery (`_GLIBCXX_BEGIN_NAMESPACE_VERSION`) provides the recovery
path: `std::__9::snzi` coexists with `std::__8::snzi`. We design
assuming this won't be needed.


Initial benchmarks {#bench}
==================

Methodology {#bench-method}
-----------

Implementation: header-only template instantiation; no out-of-line
symbols. Built with GCC 15.2 against libstdc++ -std=c++26.
Benchmark harness: Google Benchmark v1.9.4. Workload: each iteration
performs `s.arrive(); s.depart();` — a balanced pair so the indicator
ends each iteration in a consistent state.

Caveats: numbers below are from a 16-core/16-thread x86-64 development
workstation with ASLR enabled and no CPU-governor pinning. They are
*directionally* correct but should not be taken as paper-quality
measurements.

The benchmark progression planned for revision 1 and beyond:

  * **Server-class x86-64** — a 2×28-core Xeon platform (56 physical
    cores / 112 hardware threads). This is the realistic
    contemporary-server datapoint, and the platform on which the
    convenience type's tree-size default should be defended for
    LWG/LEWG review.
  * **Weakly-ordered architectures** — ARMv8 (Apple Silicon and
    server-class Neoverse) and ideally POWER9 to characterize the
    memory-ordering choices ([[#order]]) on hardware where the
    differences between `release`, `acquire`, and `seq_cst` actually
    cost cycles. We are exploring access to LLNL Sierra-class
    hardware for the POWER9 datapoint.
  * **Cross-architecture comparison** of the SNZI/atomic crossover
    point — the central scalability claim should be demonstrated on
    at least three architectures before the paper is ready for the
    main library group.

Comparison vs `atomic<long>` baseline {#bench-baseline}
-------------------------------------

The naive baseline — the "what you write if you don't have SNZI" —
is `atomic<long>::fetch_add` / `fetch_sub`:

<table>
<tr><th>Threads</th><th>atomic&lt;long&gt;</th><th>std::snzi</th><th>SNZI / atomic</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>8 ns</td><td>75 ns</td><td>9.5× slower</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>54 ns</td><td>292 ns</td><td>5.4× slower</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>116 ns</td><td>269 ns</td><td>2.3× slower</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>310 ns</td><td>250 ns</td><td>**0.81× — SNZI wins**</td></tr>
<tr><td>32</td><td>324 ns</td><td>245 ns</td><td>**0.76× — SNZI wins**</td></tr>
</table>

The crossover at 16 threads is the central scalability story. On
larger machines we expect the crossover to remain in the same
neighborhood (it is governed by atomic-counter contention, which
saturates at relatively low thread counts) but the SNZI-side
advantage past the knee should deepen as the tree's work-spreading
benefit dominates.

Tree-size sweep {#bench-treesize}
---------------

Same arrive/depart workload, varying the tree size at fixed thread
counts. Drives the [[#abi]] default-lockdown decision.

At 32 threads:

<table>
<tr><th>Tree size</th><th>ns/op</th><th>M ops/s</th></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>655</td><td>3.1</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>414</td><td>4.8</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>224</td><td>**8.9 — knee**</td></tr>
<tr><td>32</td><td>229</td><td>8.7 (plateau)</td></tr>
<tr><td>64</td><td>223</td><td>9.0 (plateau)</td></tr>
<tr><td>128</td><td>223</td><td>9.0 (plateau)</td></tr>
</table>

Tree size 16 is the knee on this machine. Tree size 32 sits well into
the plateau and is the proposed default for the convenience type,
pending validation on higher-core-count hardware.

Query throughput {#bench-query}
----------------

Single-threaded query against a busy writer: 0.365 ns/op (~2.7 G
ops/s) — essentially a single load-acquire. Exactly what the
read-mostly use cases ([[#usecases]]) want.


Integration with proposed `<rcu>` and `<hazptr>` {#integration}
================================================

These are in flight in libstdc++ at the time of writing. SNZI is a
plausible substrate for both:

  * **`<rcu>`** — readers register presence on entry to a critical
    section, deregister on exit. Writers want to test "are any old
    readers still active?" The natural use is one SNZI per epoch
    generation (low-level tier, indicator co-located with the
    generation word). SNZI replaces the per-thread quiescent-state
    machinery for the simple case.
  * **`<hazptr>`** — each hazard-pointer slot tracks an active hazard.
    Reclamation needs to test "is this object hazard-protected by
    anyone?" SNZI per protected object would be too expensive; SNZI
    per hazard slot is the natural granularity.

Issue: the precise integration story depends on what API the in-flight
`<rcu>` and `<hazptr>` headers settle on. The Root concept's
vocabulary should be chosen to compose with whatever those headers
land on. This is an active discussion item.


Open questions {#open}
==============

  1. **`arrive(0)` / `depart(0)`** — no-op or precondition violation?
     We lean toward no-op (matches the intuition of "loop body that
     happens to compute n=0 sometimes"), at the cost of one branch on
     the fast path.
  2. **`alignas(64)` vs `alignas(128)`** — the latter is more future-
     proof for Apple Silicon and recent Intel but doubles memory
     footprint. Pending data from those targets.
  3. **Tree-size default** — 32 looks right for ≤32-thread machines;
     pending data from ≥64-thread machines.
  4. **Leaf-selection on freestanding** — `std::hash<thread::id>` is
     hosted-only. We propose a pluggable leaf selector (template
     parameter) on the low-level tier with a hosted-default selector;
     freestanding callers parameterize as needed.
  5. **`query` semantics under racing arrive/depart** — `query` is
     specified to return the indicator's value at *some point* during
     the call. Stronger guarantees (e.g. linearizability with respect
     to all in-flight operations) are tempting but expensive. We lean
     toward the weak guarantee.
  6. **Wait-predicate strategy on C++20 baseline** — exact
     implementation if [[P2643]] facilities are not yet available.
     Acceptable to spin a small number of times before issuing
     `atomic_wait` to filter version-bit churn?
  7. **Bikeshed naming** — `snzi`, `nz_indicator`, or
     `scalable_nz_indicator`? `arrive`/`depart` (matching
     `latch`/`barrier` vocabulary) or `inc`/`dec`? Defer to LEWG
     once the design is otherwise settled.
  8. **Movability of the convenience type** — locked at non-movable
     in this draft. Argument for revisiting if a use case emerges
     where ownership transfer is genuinely safe (probably never).


Proposed wording {#word}
================

Issue: Pending design lockdown. The wording will be developed against
the API surface described in [[#api]] once SG1 is broadly aligned on
the design choices.


